        <p class="story-kicker">BUSINESS</p>
        <h1 class="article-title">Airline Introduces Basic Economy Plus Regret</h1>
        <p class="article-dek">New tier includes seat, bag, and a complimentary moment realizing you saved twelve dollars.</p>
        <p class="article-meta">By Marcus Tweed · Wednesday, August 19, 2026 · DALLAS · 3 min read</p>
        
<div class="article-share" aria-label="Share this story">
  <span class="article-share-label">Share</span>
  <a href="mailto:?subject=Airline%20Introduces%20Basic%20Economy%20Plus%20Regret&amp;body=https%3A//theassociatedguess.com/article/airline-introduces-basic-economy-plus-regret/">Email</a>
  <a href="https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?url=https%3A//theassociatedguess.com/article/airline-introduces-basic-economy-plus-regret/&amp;text=Airline%20Introduces%20Basic%20Economy%20Plus%20Regret" rel="noopener">Post</a>
  <a href="../../reprints.html">Reprints</a>
</div>
        
        <figure class="article-hero article-hero-fit-contain">
          <img src="https://theassociatedguess.com/assets/images/airline-introduces-basic-economy-plus-regret.png" alt="" />
        </figure>
        <div class="ad-slot ad-slot-in-content" data-ad-slot="inContent"></div>
        <div class="article-body">
          <p>DALLAS  -  SkyMerge Airlines has taken its no-nonsense approach to air travel to new lows with the introduction of Basic Economy Plus Regret, a fare class that costs $12 less than standard economy while providing &quot;the full emotional arc of modern travel,&quot; according to vice president of ancillary feelings Gil Marr.</p>
<p>The new class is designed for those who want to save money but are willing to accept the full range of emotions that come with it. Passengers board last, sit near the lavatory that “always smells like ambition,” and receive a snack mix scientifically formulated to crumble into laptop keys. Regret manifests during drink service when crew announce that the beverage cart accepts only exact change and dignity.</p>
<p>&quot;We monetized the sigh,&quot; Marr said in an interview with The Associated Guess. &quot;Investors love recurring disappointment.&quot;</p>
<p>The airline app now features a Regret Meter that fills as users decline upgrades. At 100%, it offers a meditation clip narrated by a pilot who admits he also flies middle seat on his days off.</p>
<p>Consumer advocates called the tier predatory, but SkyMerge noted that “predatory is strong language for a company that still prints boarding passes on thermal paper that fades like hope.”</p>
<p>Early adopters report the product delivers as promised. &quot;I saved enough for parking,&quot; said traveler Elena Platt. &quot;I lost enough for therapy.&quot;</p>
<p>SkyMerge plans to expand the Regret Meter into a loyalty program where repeat Regret customers earn points redeemable for slightly less Regret, subject to blackout dates and the human condition.</p>
<p>The airline has also introduced a new feature called “Regret Alerts,” which sends passengers notifications when they might be about to make a mistake. For example, if a passenger tries to use a credit card on the SkyMerge app, they receive an alert: &quot;Did you know that using your credit card for this purchase will result in 10% more Regret?&quot;</p>
<p>SkyMerge’s marketing team has even created a series of instructional videos titled “How to Maximize Your Regret.” In one video, a spokesperson explains how to navigate the beverage cart with dignity and exact change. &quot;Remember,&quot; she says, &quot;the key is to look like you&#x27;re enjoying your drink while secretly cursing the sky.&quot;</p>
<p>Local travel agents are divided on the new fare class. Some see it as an opportunity for increased bookings, while others worry about customer satisfaction. One agent, Sarah Johnson, said, “I can’t imagine selling this to a client who’s already stressed about their upcoming vacation.”</p>
<p>Despite the mixed reactions, SkyMerge is pushing forward with plans to roll out Basic Economy Plus Regret on all domestic flights by the end of the year. The airline hopes that by fully embracing regret as part of the travel experience, they will not only increase profits but also create a new standard for customer service in the industry.</p>
<p>As Marr puts it, “We’re not just selling tickets; we’re selling the entire emotional journey.” And if that means more customers are left with a lingering sense of disappointment and frustration, well, that’s the price of progress.</p>
          
        </div>
        
